<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iberian Union</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1580">1580</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Iberian Union was the dynastic union of the crowns of Portugal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> under the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> kings, beginning in 1580 and lasting until 1640. Portugal kept its own institutions but was drawn into Spain's wars. Its overseas possessions were attacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English, a significant phase in the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iberian-union.jpg" alt="Iberian Union" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gabriel Ziegler · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IberianUnionandCouncils.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alcacer-quibir">Battle of Alcácer Quibir</a> in 1578 killed the young King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebastian-of-portugal">Sebastian of Portugal</a>. His granduncle and successor, Cardinal Henry, was 66, and his death was followed by a succession crisis in which three grandchildren of Manuel I claimed the throne: Infanta Catarina, Duchess of Braganza, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-prior-of-crato">António, Prior of Crato</a>, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a>. António was acclaimed king at Santarém on 24 July 1580 and then in many other towns, but members of the Council of Governors who backed Philip fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and declared him the legal successor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Philip marched into Portugal and defeated the troops loyal to António at the Battle of Alcântara. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-alvarez-de-toledo-3rd-duke-of-alba">Duke of Alba</a> imposed Philip's rule on the provinces before entering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, where he seized a great treasure and let his soldiers sack the surroundings. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes-of-tomar">Cortes of Tomar</a> recognised Philip as king in 1581, and he left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> in 1583, naming his nephew Albert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> viceroy. Under the pledges made at Tomar, Portugal kept its own law, currency and government, and offices at home and overseas were reserved for Portuguese subjects. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-portugal">Council of Portugal</a> advised the king in Madrid. In the sixty years of the union the country had 13 viceroys and four regency councils.</p><p>The union brought Portugal into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s wars. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> treated it as a reason to attack Portuguese colonies, and the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a> followed the war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The Dutch invaded parts of Asia, including Portuguese Ceylon, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> they took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recife">Recife</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olinda">Olinda</a> in 1630. John Maurice of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegen">Siegen</a>, governor of the Dutch possessions from 1637, also took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elmina">Elmina Castle</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-tome">São Tomé</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a>. The Dutch occupation of Brazil ended after the Second Battle of Guararapes.</p><p>Under Philip III of Portugal, taxes fell mainly on Portuguese merchants, Spaniards took government posts and the nobility lost influence. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaspar-de-guzman-count-duke-of-olivares">Count-Duke of Olivares</a> pressed for uniformity and for Portugal to share <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>'s military burdens. On 1 December 1640 the Forty Conspirators killed the Secretary of State Miguel de Vasconcelos, imprisoned the vicereine Margaret of Savoy and acclaimed John, 8th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iv-of-portugal">Duke of Braganza</a>, as John IV.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Restoration War that followed consisted mainly of skirmishes near the border, with Portuguese victories at Elvas in 1659, Ameixial in 1663, Castelo Rodrigo in 1664 and Montes Claros in 1665. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> recognised Portuguese sovereignty and made peace on 13 February 1668. The city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a>, once Portuguese, stayed with Spain, and its coat of arms is nearly identical to that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Iberian Union” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Iberian Union</b> of 1580 preserved Portuguese laws and institutions but exposed Portugal’s overseas empire to the consequences of Spain’s wars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Their victory at Alcácer Quibir had consequences far beyond Morocco: the death of the childless Sebastian led to a succession crisis that ended with Philip II of Spain taking the Portuguese crown in 1580, beginning the <b>Iberian Union</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saadi-sultanate">Saadi Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
